WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.698
Two shore boats went ashore from the Hispaniola while the captain, the squire, and I talked matters over in the cabin.

2
00:00:07.699 --> 00:00:16.853
"If there were a breath of wind, we could overpower the mutineers still on the ship, cut loose the anchor, and sail away," the captain said.

3
00:00:16.854 --> 00:00:19.689
Then Hunter came in and shut the door behind him.

4
00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:25.912
"Jim Hawkins was on one of those boats; he’s gone ashore with the rest," he said.

5
00:00:25.913 --> 00:00:32.594
"Even wind won’t help us now. We can’t leave," the captain said. "I fear for his safety."

6
00:00:32.595 --> 00:00:37.003
"We may never see the lad again," the squire said.

7
00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:42.761
We all went up on deck, and there was a nasty stench of illness in the air that made me sick.

8
00:00:42.762 --> 00:00:48.887
The six remaining scoundrels were sitting and grumbling under a sail in the forecastle.

9
00:00:48.888 --> 00:00:55.501
Ashore, we could see the two boats tied up, with one man sitting in each, near the mouth of the river.

10
00:00:55.502 --> 00:01:00.284
"I can’t just sit here," I said. "I’m going ashore in the rowboat." "I’m

11
00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:02.733
going with you," Hunter said. The

12
00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:09.146
map had indicated a stockade, and Hunter and I steered the rowboat in its direction. The

13
00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:16.349
two men guarding the shore boats seemed anxious at our appearance; I could see the pair discussing what they ought to do. But

14
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:21.609
they had their orders, I suppose, and decided to stay where they were. There

15
00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:26.799
was a slight bend in the shoreline, and I steered out of their line of sight. When

16
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.284
we landed, I jumped out and ran, with a pistol in each hand. I

17
00:01:31.285 --> 00:01:35.915
had hardly gone a hundred yards when I reached the stockade. Inside

18
00:01:35.916 --> 00:01:42.828
a six-foot-high fence was a sturdy log house with a spring of clear water that rose up underneath it. The

19
00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:49.758
house could hold thirty people in a pinch, and there were some holes in the walls for men with muskets to defend the stockade. Around

20
00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:59.514
the building was a wide space that inclined upward, so whoever controlled the log house had every advantage over any attackers. "This

21
00:01:59.515 --> 00:02:03.621
spring is particularly advantageous," I thought. "We

22
00:02:03.622 --> 00:02:10.014
have arms and ammunition on the Hispaniola , things to eat, and excellent wines—but no water."

23
00:02:10.015 --> 00:02:18.447
Just then I heard the cry of a man at the point of death ringing over the island, followed by the distinct clap of a pistol.

24
00:02:18.448 --> 00:02:23.012
"Jim Hawkins is dead," I thought.

25
00:02:23.013 --> 00:02:31.428
Immediately, I returned to the shore and jumped in the rowboat, and Hunter made the water fly until the boat was soon alongside the Hispaniola

26
00:02:31.429 --> 00:02:35.406
. When I was back aboard the ship, I found everyone

27
00:02:35.407 --> 00:02:43.327
shaken. The squire had his head in his hands, and one of the six crewmen seemed tortured by guilt as

28
00:02:43.328 --> 00:02:49.669
well. "That one," Captain Smollett said, nodding toward him, "nearly fainted when he heard the

29
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:53.178
gunshot. One little push and that man will join our side."

30
00:02:53.179 --> 00:02:59.416
I told the captain about my plan to take refuge inside the stockade, and we settled on the details:

31
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:04.763
Redruth would stay in the galley with loaded muskets for protection.

32
00:03:04.764 --> 00:03:07.879
Hunter brought the boat just off the stern,

33
00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:16.650
and Joyce and I started loading powder tins, muskets, bags of biscuits, pork, some wine, and my invaluable medicine chest.

34
00:03:16.651 --> 00:03:20.749
In the meantime, the squire and the captain stayed on deck.

35
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:29.981
"Mr. Hands," the captain called out, "two of us have our pistols out and ready. If anyone signals Silver, you’re all dead."

36
00:03:29.982 --> 00:03:37.708
"We’ll see about that," said one of the men, and they ran below deck to the galley, where Redruth was waiting for them.

37
00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:46.972
The crewmen scattered throughout the ship, and every time one popped his head out of an opening, the captain would point his pistol and say, "Down, dog!"

38
00:03:46.973 --> 00:03:56.976
By this time, we had the rowboat loaded as much as we dared, and Joyce, Hunter, and I quietly made for shore as fast as the oars could take us.

39
00:03:56.977 --> 00:04:03.173
This second trip alarmed the lookouts on the beach, and one of them disappeared into the woods.

40
00:04:03.174 --> 00:04:12.933
We soon touched land and transferred everything into the stockade, and Hunter and Joyce stayed to guard the house while I rowed back to the Hispaniola

41
00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:20.555
. The squire, who was waiting, caught the boat and secured it, and we started loading it with more

42
00:04:20.556 --> 00:04:25.644
provisions. After the squire, Redruth, the captain, and I each took a musket and a

43
00:04:25.645 --> 00:04:30.734
cutlass, we dumped the rest of the arms and powder near the cabin overboard into the

44
00:04:30.735 --> 00:04:36.791
sea. By this time, we heard sailors faintly calling to each other from the direction of the two

45
00:04:36.792 --> 00:04:41.620
boats. "Joyce and Hunter should be safe," I said, "but we must

46
00:04:41.621 --> 00:04:46.408
hurry!" Redruth dropped into the boat, which we then brought around for the

47
00:04:46.409 --> 00:04:50.986
captain. "Now, men," Smollett said to the rest of the crew, "do you hear me?"

48
00:04:50.987 --> 00:04:54.107
There was no answer from the forecastle.

49
00:04:54.108 --> 00:04:57.919
"It’s up to you, Abe Gray—it’s to you I am speaking."

50
00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.881
Still no reply.

51
00:05:00.882 --> 00:05:09.331
"Gray," Captain Smollett resumed, a little louder, "I’m leaving this ship, and I order you to follow your captain.

52
00:05:09.332 --> 00:05:14.670
You are a good man at heart. In fact, not one of you is as bad as he makes out.

53
00:05:14.671 --> 00:05:20.906
I give you thirty seconds to join me." He paused and waited for a reply.

54
00:05:20.907 --> 00:05:25.349
"Come, my fine fellow," the captain continued. "Don’t wait.

55
00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:30.225
I’m risking my life and the lives of these good gentlemen every second."

56
00:05:30.226 --> 00:05:36.533
There was a sudden racket, a sound of blows, and out burst Abe Gray with blood on his cheek.

57
00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:41.130
"I’m with you, sir!" he yelled, running to the captain.

58
00:05:41.131 --> 00:05:44.324
He and the captain got aboard with us, and we shoved off.

59
00:05:44.325 --> 00:05:52.759
We were off the ship, but not yet safe inside our stockade.

